   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the executive law, in relation to human rights violations by a\nreal estate appraiser",
    "basePrintNo": "S7175",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7175-2023",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §160-u, Exec L",
    "lawSection": "Executive Law",
    "memo": " \nBILL NUMBER: S7175\n\nSPONSOR: BAILEY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the executive law, in relation to human rights\nviolations by a real estate appraiser\n\n \nPURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA:\n\nThis bill gives the Department of State greater discretion in disciplin-\ning a real estate broker or salesman who violates provisions of the\nstate Human Rights Law\n\n \nSUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one of the bill adds a new subsection (m) which adds a violation\nof article fifteen of the executive law to the list of violations that\ncould cause a possible revocation of suspension of a real estate\nlicense.\n\nSection two of the bill sets forth the effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nTo avoid discriminatory behavior by real estate appraisers and ensure\nequitable treatment to all homeowners, we must create a level playing\nfield. There is compelling evidence that real estate appraisers in Illi-\nnois, California, Florida, and Connecticut have been found to lower\nappraised home values by up to 50% due to what is found inside a home.\nItems such as cultural pieces, books written by people of color and\nfamily photos of people of color were found to lower home values when\nvisible in plain view compared to when they were removed.  We need to\nensure that this discriminatory practice does not take place in New York\nby enacting legislation that discourages this act.  This issue has also\nbeen considered relevant in Congress; a bill has been proposed which\nwould create a task force on this 'topic and launch a program to enhance\ndiversity in this profession.\n\nArticle 15 of the Executive Law, known as the New York State Human\nRights Law, prohibits discrimination on the basis of a protected charac-\nteristic, including race, creed, national origin, sex, age, disability,\nmarital status, military status, family status, sexual orientation, or\ngender identity. Providing the Department of State with the additional\nauthority to revoke, or suspend the license of a real estate appraiser\nfound in violation of the Human Rights Law will signal that discrimi-\nnation of any kind is unacceptable in New York.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nNew bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATION FOR STATE AND LOCAL GOVERNMENTS:\n\nNone.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately.\n",
